text_bindings.text:
The source uses a hydrogen-bomb explosion as an example, giving a released energy of nearly $10^{17}$ joules and saying that this exceeds the electrical energy generated worldwide over several days. That comparison retains the source's historical context;
it is not presented as a current statistic.

The released energy travels with radiation. Under the source's mass-energy relation, the energy carried by the radiation has a mass equivalent of about $0.1\%$ of the original material's mass;
this mass equivalent is not the radiation particles' invariant rest mass.
